Read a lot acceptance plan through its OC curve (@cm-qualification-lot: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @clean-manufacturing, #source "../../../inquiry/metrology/sources/Statistical-Handbook.lingua#r_6S3221KSX1KKGPJNXKC3N022NN", #clean-manufacturing "Account-for-finite-lots-and-imperfect-classification.lingua#r_1HE80AB3137D571YYYFQ67Z64N") { r_6373FJ8A59PDWJGD2P2QW3NJ5F
**Read:** [Measurement Process Characterization — NIST/SEMATECH](../../../inquiry/metrology/sources/Statistical-Handbook.lingua#r_6S3221KSX1KKGPJNXKC3N022NN), sections 6.2.1 and [6.2.3.2, choosing a plan with an OC curve](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section2/pmc232.htm).

For independent Bernoulli classifications with nonconforming fraction p, a plan observes n items and accepts when at most c are nonconforming. Its operating characteristic is P_accept(p) = Σ from d=0 to c of C(n,d)p^d(1−p)^(n−d). This is a property of the sampling rule under its model, not a promise about one accepted lot.

Reconstruct the handbook's n = 52, c = 3 example. At p = 0.03, acceptance probability is about 0.9295374. Producer and consumer risks refer to specified population states and decisions; the two reference quality levels do not assert that every accepted lot has a particular quality.

Under the handbook's additional ideal rectifying-inspection assumptions and N = 10,000, the corresponding approximate average outgoing fraction is 0.0277411 and expected inspection count is 752.9621. These depend on complete successful screening of rejected lots. Recompute the printed tables: at p = 0.04 and 0.05 the binomial formula gives approximately 0.8459895 and 0.7383174, which do not round to the page's displayed 0.845 and 0.739. Preserve the model and calculation when checking a published result.

**Work:** Plot the OC curve and compare another plan. Show how inspection error or selection bias changes the decision. Distinguish quality estimation, lot disposition and sustained process improvement.

